# RoomFlow line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#   This repository is developed on Windows workstations, where the global
#   gitconfig sets `core.autocrlf=true`, and deployed by shipping files to a
#   LINUX staging host that runs them. With no attributes, a Windows checkout of
#   docker/health-check.sh contains CRLF and the host therefore reads the second
#   line as `set -euo pipefail\r` — an invalid shell option — so the post-deploy
#   health check dies instantly. The deploy step then reports the new release as
#   unhealthy and rolls it back: a CRLF checkout could roll back a perfectly good
#   deploy. `eol=lf` makes the checked-out bytes identical on every OS.
#
# SCOPE
#   Deliberately narrow: only the text files this deployment actually executes,
#   parses, or feeds to `docker compose --env-file`. There is intentionally NO
#   blanket `* text=auto` rule — that would renormalise every file in the
#   repository and produce a large, unrelated diff; this change does not rewrite
#   the repo's line endings.
#
#   Deliberately NOT included: `*.md` (docs are rendered by GitHub, which handles
#   CRLF), and the frontend/Java sources (their toolchains are line-ending
#   agnostic, and neither is copied straight into a shell or a config parser).
#   Binary files are untouched — the patterns below are all text extensions.
#
# `text` + `eol=lf` normalises on commit as well: the blob is stored with LF and
# every future checkout gets LF, on Windows included.

# Executed by bash on the staging host, via `bash docker/health-check.sh` and
# over SSH. CRLF here is the failure mode described above.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Compose files, the GitHub Actions deploy workflow, and other YAML. Parsed on
# the runner and on the Linux host.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# .env templates (.env.staging.example is the tracked staging template). A stray
# CR would end up INSIDE an interpolated value — e.g. a password or a BCrypt
# hash — and silently change the secret.
*.example text eol=lf

# docker/nginx.conf is COPYed into the frontend image and read by nginx on
# Linux, where a trailing CR becomes part of the directive value.
*.conf text eol=lf

# Keep the policy files themselves LF so they stay portable.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
